+/* Ultrawide HUD: is this draw part of the 2D overlay, to be drawn at its own
+ * proportions instead of stretched across the widened picture? Yes when the
+ * layout is not "stretched", the surface is widened, the draw goes into a
+ * display buffer without a depth test (2D draws with one are the story
+ * scenes' speech bubbles, see below), its vertex program is the plain 2D
+ * kind (ld_vs_position_rows) with an orthographic w (overlays fixed to the
+ * world, like enemies' gauges, have a perspective one and stay with the
+ * picture), and its vertices do not span the screen -- full-screen passes
+ * (the resolve, bloom, fades, menu backdrops) keep covering all of it.
+ * `viewportTransform` is the draw's xf (x scale and offset at 0 and 4, y at
+ * 1 and 5).
+ *
+ * Returns 0 for "leave it", else where it goes: LD_HUD_CENTRE into the middle
+ * 16:9, LD_HUD_LEFT / LD_HUD_RIGHT pushed out towards that edge (the
+ * "edges" layout). Folklore's HUD (capture-20261031-111758): the map and its
+ * icons centred around x 80-500, y 0-330 of the 1280x720 screen; the health
+ * and ID gauges and the folk diamond inside x 744-1200, y 60-300; the button
+ * hint text at x 1020-1130, y 614-650. So: a draw centred in the top-left
+ * (x <= 560, y <= 432) goes left, one centred in the top-right (x >= 700) or
+ * the bottom-right corner (x >= 960, y >= 560) goes right, everything else
+ * stays centred. Text comes one letter per draw: a letter
+ * that carries on the line of the previous one (same pipeline, overlapping
+ * it vertically, starting just after it) goes where that one went, so a
+ * line of text is never split between two places. */
